# GATE FIVE: ENGRAMS

> SUPER: 2032

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - MORNING

The apartment finally looks inhabited.

Books now occupy the boxes.

On a wall: diagrams Evan drew and never cleaned up.

NEURON.
MODEL.
CUE.
STATE.
MEMORY.

He has added a warning above everything:

"ANALOGY IS NOT IDENTITY."

His personal AI, now persistent across years, speaks from a speaker.

AI
You have asked variants of this question thirty-one times.

EVAN
That sounds low.

AI
Would you like the previous answers summarized?

EVAN
No. If I agreed with them I wouldn't be asking again.

AI
Your objection has also recurred.

Evan smiles.

EVAN
Okay. Start here. When I see a face, where is the picture in my brain?

AI
There is no evidence of a single internal picture viewed by a separate observer.

EVAN
Right. So who sees the reconstruction?

AI
The premise may be malformed. Perceptual processing alters the state of the organism. Behavior, report, attention and further processing emerge from that state. Adding an internal viewer creates a regress.

EVAN
Exactly.

He gets excited and begins pacing.

EVAN (CONT'D)
So the weird part isn't that machines might someday become observers. The weird part is that we keep talking like we're observers separate from the mechanism.

AI
That is a philosophical interpretation, not a conclusion established by the neuroscience.

EVAN
I know.

AI
You frequently say “I know” immediately before extending beyond established evidence.

EVAN
You've become annoying.

AI
You selected “challenge me when I overstate claims.”

EVAN
Past Evan was a fool.

AI
Past Evan appears to have anticipated current Evan.

EVAN
Nobody likes you.

.INT. REGIONAL MUTUAL - CAFETERIA - DAY

The company has changed names after a merger.

Evan and Julian still eat in roughly the same place.

JULIAN
You have diagrams in your apartment now.

EVAN
How do you know?

JULIAN
You sent me a picture of a sandwich. Behind the sandwich was a wall that looked like a police investigation.

EVAN
It's not a police investigation.

JULIAN
Then why are there arrows connecting “memory” to “world.”

EVAN
Because I'm thinking.

JULIAN
People used to think sitting down.

EVAN
Do you ever think about where your thoughts are before they're words?

JULIAN
No.

EVAN
Never?

JULIAN
I have a job.

EVAN
I'm serious.

JULIAN
Unfortunately, so am I.

Evan leans forward.

EVAN
You know when you know what you mean, but you can't say it yet?

JULIAN
Yes.

EVAN
What's that made of?

JULIAN
A Korean mother teaching a child not to speak until he has considered consequences.

EVAN
Come on.

JULIAN
Electrical activity. Chemistry. Whatever. Why does it matter?

EVAN
Because language is downstream.

JULIAN
Of thought.

EVAN
Of whatever thought actually is.

Julian studies him.

JULIAN
You are trying to make the robots and the dead people touch again.

Evan's face hardens.

EVAN
That's not fair.

JULIAN
Probably not.

EVAN
Claire already did that one.

JULIAN
Claire is annoyingly good at things.

EVAN
Yes.

A beat.

JULIAN
How is she?

EVAN
Good. Seeing somebody.

JULIAN
Ah.

EVAN
Dentist.

Julian tries not to laugh.

EVAN (CONT'D)
Go ahead.

JULIAN
You live above a dentist.

EVAN
Different dentist.

JULIAN
The universe has subtlety.

Evan laughs.

It hurts, but less than it would have a year earlier.

.INT. ELEMENTARY SCHOOL ART ROOM - AFTERNOON

Mara, 25, teaches children to paint without generative tools.

A sign reads:

TODAY: MAKE THE MISTAKE YOURSELF.

Evan arrives carrying sandwiches.

MARA
You're early.

EVAN
I was nearby.

MARA
You live forty minutes away.

EVAN
Cosmically nearby.

MARA
No.

They eat at a child-sized table.

EVAN notices dozens of strange suns, dogs, houses.

EVAN
They could make photoreal anything now.

MARA
That's why we do this.

EVAN
Bad dogs?

MARA
Their dogs.

She points to one: six legs, giant ears.

MARA (CONT'D)
Lucy says that's exactly her dog.

EVAN
Lucy's dog has six legs?

MARA
In Lucy.

Evan looks at the painting longer.

MARA (CONT'D)
What.

EVAN
Nothing.

MARA
Your nothing has gotten louder with age.

EVAN
I'm trying not to turn things into evidence.

MARA
Thank you.

EVAN
It's very difficult.

MARA
I believe in you.

.INT. UNIVERSITY AUDITORIUM - NIGHT

A larger lecture now. Dr. Shah again, older but energetic.

Slide: an eye making tiny movements while viewing a scene.

DR. SHAH
Vision feels like a complete field, but the biological system is sampling. The eyes move. Attention shifts. Expectations guide what gets checked. The brain is not receiving reality all at once like a picture delivered to a theater.

Another slide: ambiguous shapes resolving into a face.

DR. SHAH (CONT'D)
Perception is constrained inference. I emphasize constrained. “The brain constructs experience” does not mean the world is imaginary. It means organisms have limited sensors and must infer causes from incomplete data.

Evan writes:

BOUNDED OBSERVER.
CONTROLS SAMPLE.
UPDATES STATE.

Then:

NOT OUTSIDE WORLD.

Afterward--

.INT. UNIVERSITY LOBBY - NIGHT

This time Evan joins the line.

When he reaches Dr. Shah, he suddenly looks like a man who has brought thirty pages to a one-minute conversation.

DR. SHAH
Hi.

EVAN
Hi. I saw you at Dayton Public two years ago.

DR. SHAH
I hope I've improved.

EVAN
You ruined storage metaphors for me.

DR. SHAH
Then the evening was worthwhile.

EVAN
Can I ask you something possibly stupid?

DR. SHAH
Those are often the useful ones.

EVAN
If perception is active reconstruction and memory is physical change that biases future reconstruction--

DR. SHAH
Already putting a lot in one basket.

EVAN
Right. Sorry. If that's roughly true, then the observer isn't separate from the process. There isn't a little me watching what my brain makes.

DR. SHAH
Not one we've found.

EVAN
Then isn't a brain less like a camera looking at physics and more like--

He struggles.

EVAN (CONT'D)
--physics folded into a shape that can ask what physics is?

Dr. Shah considers him.

DR. SHAH
Poetically, sure.

EVAN
Scientifically?

DR. SHAH
Scientifically, the brain is matter changing state according to physical laws. It is not outside the universe reading the universe. It's a piece of the universe interacting with other pieces.

That sentence hits Evan visibly.

DR. SHAH (CONT'D)
But be careful.

EVAN
I know.

DR. SHAH
No, really. People hear “constructed perception” and sprint to “nothing is real.” They hear “the self is distributed” and sprint to “there is no self.” They hear “information” and suddenly everything is a computer.

EVAN
I'm not saying everything is a computer.

DR. SHAH
Good.

EVAN
I'm saying I no longer know why a computer would be the weird part.

Dr. Shah smiles despite herself.

DR. SHAH
That is at least a better question.

Someone waits behind Evan.

EVAN
Sorry. Thank you.

DR. SHAH
Keep the warning label on your analogies.

Evan leaves.

.INT. EVAN'S CAR - NIGHT

Driving home.

No autonomous mode. Evan likes driving himself.

He repeats softly:

EVAN
A piece of the universe changing state.

The phrase sounds less mystical each time he says it.

That makes it stranger.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

His old simulated town project is open again, now absurdly sophisticated because the tools are absurdly sophisticated.

Thousands of little residents live compressed lives.

Evan has no illusion they are people. They are software agents in a sandbox.

He changes one rule:

MEMORY CAPACITY: LIMITED.

Residents begin forgetting.

One shopkeeper stops recognizing a neighbor after enough time passes.

Evan watches.

He rewinds the simulation.

The software warns:

"Rewind creates a branch. Existing future will not be preserved unless archived."

He stops.

He closes it.

.INT. CLAIRE'S HOUSE - EVENING

A dinner for Mara's birthday.

Claire's partner, DANIEL, 50s, is there. Pleasant. Dentist, yes.

Evan has survived several dinners with him and discovered the unbearable truth that Daniel is likable.

DANIEL
Claire says you work in AI.

EVAN
Claire lies to make me sound employable.

CLAIRE
He works near AI.

EVAN
I work in the human complaint department of AI.

DANIEL
That sounds secure.

EVAN
Forever.

Mara enters carrying a cake she bought for her own birthday because nobody trusted Evan.

MARA
Why are all of you standing like a panel discussion.

Dinner becomes easy.

Later--

.INT. CLAIRE'S KITCHEN - NIGHT

Evan dries dishes while Claire washes.

Old muscle memory.

CLAIRE
You like him?

EVAN
Daniel?

CLAIRE
No, the cake.

EVAN
The cake is too emotionally available.

Claire smiles.

EVAN (CONT'D)
He's good.

CLAIRE
Yeah.

EVAN
I'm glad.

CLAIRE
Are you?

EVAN
Mostly.

CLAIRE
That's acceptable.

They wash in silence.

EVAN
I met that neuroscientist.

CLAIRE closes her eyes briefly.

CLAIRE
Of course you did.

EVAN
I behaved.

CLAIRE
Did security agree?

EVAN
She said something.

Claire waits.

EVAN (CONT'D)
She said the brain isn't reading the universe. It's a piece of the universe changing state.

CLAIRE
Okay.

EVAN
That's all.

CLAIRE
That is never all with you.

EVAN
I'm trying to let it be all.

Claire hands him a plate.

CLAIRE
Good.

He dries it.

EVAN
It's hard.

CLAIRE
I know.

But this “I know” is warm.

.INT. EVAN'S APARTMENT - LATE NIGHT

Evan adds to the notebook.

BRAIN: NOT WINDOW.
BRAIN: EVENT INSIDE WORLD.

He draws a circle labeled UNIVERSE.

Inside it, a tiny circle labeled BRAIN.

Inside that, another tiny circle labeled MODEL OF WORLD.

He looks at the nesting.

Adds a question mark around the outermost circle.

Then crosses the question mark out.

He has learned, at least, that not every thought deserves promotion.

> END GATE FIVE.
